{
  "$comment": "The flat views read off a knowledge graph, as every implementation must read them: a graph, the id of its main resource, and what the main resource is connected to, what cites it and what entity types the annotations on it carry. Semiont's TypeScript (packages/core: knowledge-graph-views.ts), which the Browser calls, Semiont's Librarian (images/librarian: views.py), and Semiont's Worker (images/worker: generation/graph_views.py), which its generation prompt reads, each run every case and fail on a difference. The Worker reads no connection's `bidirectional` and no `citedByCount`, which its prompt does not state, and is held to the rest. An implementation in another language runs the same file. WHAT A CASE STATES. `graph` is a knowledge graph: nodes that are resources or annotations, and edges that name their two ends by id. `resource` is the id of the main resource. `annotation`, where a case has one, is the id of the focal annotation: the one the views are read for. `views` is what is read: `connections`, `citedBy`, `citedByCount` and `siblingEntityTypes`. All three lists are in a stated order. THE RULE. An edge of type `annotation-of` says which resource an annotation is on: its source is on its target. A CITATION is an edge of type `cites` whose target is the main resource and whose source is on a resource, by an `annotation-of` edge stated anywhere in the graph, before it or after it. That resource cites the main resource. `citedBy` has one entry for each citing resource, in the order of the first citation from each: its id, and its name, which is its node's label, or its id where the graph has no node for it. Several citations from one resource are one entry. `citedByCount` is the length of `citedBy`. A CONNECTION is any other edge whose source is the main resource, whatever its type: an edge named `cites` that is no citation is a connection like any other. `connections` has one entry for each such edge, in the order of the edges, and two edges to one node are two entries. An entry has the id of the edge's target; the name and entity types of the target's node, or the id as its name and no entity types where the graph has no node for it, and no entity types where the node states none; and the edge's `bidirectional` flag, which is false where the edge states none. Every other edge is in neither list. `siblingEntityTypes` is the entity types of the annotation nodes that are on the main resource, but for the focal annotation: each type once, in the order the nodes first state it. An annotation on another resource adds none, though it cites the main resource, and neither does an annotation node no `annotation-of` edge places. Add a case here before changing an implementation.",
